    "passageText": "When a language is described as endangered, attention often focuses on the number of remaining speakers. Numbers matter, but they can conceal important differences. A language spoken fluently by 5,000 older adults but rarely learned by children may be more vulnerable than one spoken by a smaller community in which children use it every day. Revitalisation programmes therefore aim not only to document vocabulary but to expand the situations in which a language is used. Schools can teach reading and writing, community classes can support adult learners, and digital media can make songs, games and conversations available to younger speakers. Yet classroom knowledge does not automatically become everyday speech. Learners need opportunities to use the language with family, friends and colleagues. Standardisation can help create textbooks and keyboards, but it can also generate disagreement. A language may contain regional varieties with different pronunciations or spellings. Choosing one form for official materials can make publishing easier while causing speakers of other varieties to feel that their own speech has been treated as incorrect. Technology is similarly double-edged. Predictive text, speech recognition and online dictionaries can make a language easier to use on modern devices. However, building these tools requires large amounts of linguistic data, and smaller languages often lack the commercial market that attracts private investment. For this reason, revitalisation is as much social as technical. Recording elder speakers is valuable, but an archive alone cannot maintain a living speech community. Long-term success depends on whether people have reasons, confidence and opportunities to use the language across generations. The goal is not necessarily to recreate a past in which everyone spoke in the same way, but to make continued use possible in changing circumstances.",
