    "title": "9 — The Right to Repair",
    "passageText": "Modern consumer devices are often thinner, lighter and more tightly integrated than their predecessors. Batteries may be glued into place, components soldered together and diagnostic software restricted to authorised technicians. Manufacturers argue that such design choices can improve durability, water resistance or security. Critics respond that they can also make ordinary repairs unnecessarily difficult. The “right to repair” movement does not refer to a single policy. Proposals may require manufacturers to sell spare parts, publish repair manuals, provide diagnostic software or avoid technical locks that prevent replacement components from functioning. The details matter because access to a manual is of limited value if a crucial part cannot be purchased, while spare parts are less useful if software refuses to recognise them. Repairability also involves trade-offs. A modular phone with easily replaceable parts may be thicker than a highly integrated one. Keeping parts available for many years can impose storage and logistics costs. On the other hand, extending the useful life of devices may reduce demand for new manufacturing and can make ownership cheaper over time. Environmental claims require care. Repairing a broken screen is likely to avoid some material use, but the overall benefit depends on what would otherwise have happened. If a repaired device consumes substantially more electricity than a newer replacement, the comparison becomes more complicated. For many small electronics, however, manufacturing accounts for a significant share of lifetime environmental impact. The debate is therefore not simply between consumers who want cheap repairs and companies that want replacement sales. It concerns how design, safety, software control, environmental costs and ownership rights should be balanced. Policies that increase repair access can still leave legitimate questions about standards and responsibility for failed repairs.",
