    "title": "10 — Why Seed Banks Are Not Frozen Farms",
    "passageText": "Seed banks store seeds from crop varieties and wild relatives under cool, dry conditions so that genetic material can be preserved for future use. They are sometimes described as agricultural insurance policies. The comparison is useful, but it can give the impression that storing a packet of seeds permanently preserves a crop exactly as it exists in the field. Seeds remain living material. Even under carefully controlled conditions, their viability declines over time. Collections must therefore test germination and periodically grow plants to produce fresh seed. This regeneration is not risk-free. If only a small number of plants are grown, some genetic variation may be lost by chance. Plants may also cross-pollinate with the wrong variety unless procedures are carefully managed. Not all crops can be conserved easily as conventional seed. Some produce seeds that do not tolerate drying or freezing, while crops such as bananas are commonly propagated without seed. These plants may require field collections, tissue culture or storage of material at extremely low temperatures. Seed banks also depend on information. A sample labelled only with a species name may be far less useful than one accompanied by details about where it was collected, the climate, soil conditions and traits observed by farmers. Such information can help breeders identify material likely to contain useful characteristics such as drought tolerance or disease resistance. Conservation in a bank does not replace conservation on farms or in wild habitats. Populations growing in changing environments continue to evolve, while stored samples largely preserve variation from the time of collection. The two approaches therefore serve different purposes. A seed bank can protect material from immediate loss, but it is one component of a broader system for maintaining agricultural diversity.",
