    "title": "11 — Noise, Signal and the Temptation of Precision",
    "passageText": "Scientific instruments often display measurements with many decimal places, creating an impression of exactness. Yet the number of digits on a screen is not the same as the amount of reliable information in a measurement. A balance may report mass to 0.001 g, but vibration, temperature change or imperfect calibration can make the final digits unstable. Researchers distinguish repeatability from accuracy. A sensor can give almost the same reading every time and still be systematically wrong if it has been calibrated against an incorrect reference. Conversely, a set of readings may vary around the true value because of random noise. Repeating measurements can reduce the influence of random variation when results are averaged, but repetition does not automatically remove a consistent bias. Context also determines what level of precision matters. A one-millimetre error may be trivial when measuring a road but critical when manufacturing a tiny mechanical component. Reporting more decimal places than the method supports can therefore mislead rather than inform. Uncertainty is not an admission that measurement has failed. It is a quantitative statement about the range within which a value is reasonably believed to lie. Two measurements that differ slightly may be effectively indistinguishable if their uncertainty ranges overlap substantially. By contrast, a small numerical difference can be meaningful when uncertainty is very low. This is why good measurement involves judgement as well as instruments. Researchers must consider calibration, environmental conditions, sampling and the purpose of the measurement. Precision is valuable, but only when it corresponds to dependable information. An apparently rough result with well-characterised uncertainty can be scientifically more useful than a beautifully precise number whose final digits are noise.",
